ld have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Jump method vs false decimal.","caption":"Fourteen pairs.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">2:40→4:15</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">1 h 35 min</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">decimal subtract times</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about elapsed with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSubtracting digital times as if they were base-100 decimals (e.g. 4.15−2.40) fails when minutes borrow. Here is a more productive model: Start 9:20, end 11:05 → +1 h to 10:20 +45 min to 11:05 = 1 h 45 min. Crossing noon needs am/pm or 24-hour care.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\n1 hour 35 minutes.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Subtracting digital times as if they were base-100 decimals (e.g. 4.15−2.40) fails when minutes borrow. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about elapsed with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nElapsed time is the duration from a start time to an end time; jump hours then minutes on a number line or clock. Now choose different numbers to show you can transfer the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","explain":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Transfer to a new problem"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 30, Lesson 1: Elapsed time","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Time and timetables","week":30,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nStart 9:20, end 11:05 → +1 h to 10:20 +45 min to 11:05 = 1 h 45 min. Crossing noon needs am/pm or 24-hour care.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the places, values or distances used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Elapsed time method.","caption":"Not base-100 decimal subtract.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Jump hours</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">then minutes</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Elapsed</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">duration</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Jump method vs false decimal.","caption":"Fourteen pairs.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">2:40→4:15<